{ns}
{define: Bm base-fret 1 frets x x 4 4 3 2} 
{define: Bb base-fret 1 frets x x 3 3 3 1} 
{define: Dsus4 base-fret 1 frets x x 0 2 3 3} 
{define: D@7 base-fret 1 frets x x x 7 8 7} 
{define: F10 base-fret 1 frets x x 12 11 10 10} 
{define: Gadd base-fret 1 frets 3 2 0 0 3 3} 
{t: WHEN YOU’RE NEXT TO ME (In D) – Mitch & Mickey }
{st: (Levy) }
{artist: Mitch & Mickey }
{composer: Eugene Levy }
 

https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a4sVu0z5Qo8  (Capo 0) (MC0)

There’s also a version in G which may be a little easier to play although you'll have to capo at the 7th fret to play along with the video.

TIP:  If transposing, play the root chord of the transposed (Gadd) and add the 5th note as necessary.   

TIP:  In the third sequence of the intro after the (D) just hold form and slide up to the 7th fret (it should be marked) and back down again.  (The D@7 won't transpose.)
 
TIP:  In the bridge there are single strums of the |A|-|Asus4|-|A|.  While playing (A) just add your pinky to the 2nd string/3rd fret (or slide your ring finger up fret) and then reverse.  On/off (or up/down).
 
TIP:  The last chord is a (D).  If you can reach it and want to get fancy play an (F) shape up on the 10th fret.  (The F10 won't transpose.) 
   
   
